WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.240
- [Narrator] We've all been here.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.710
You wanna stream a show,
but when you go to load it,

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.670
it does this.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.420
In the age of high speed internet

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.970
the circle can be maddening.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.410
But before you call your provider

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:16.340
know that you're probably already paying

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:19.570
for faster internet speeds
than you actually use.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.603
We'll explain.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:26.120
This is your computer, it
connects to your router.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.240
Your router is one of many routers

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.950
which make up your provider's network.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.920
Your provider's network
is one of more than 70,000

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.990
such networks that make up the internet.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.660
When you try to load a video
or type really anything

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:40.870
into your browser,

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.990
your router sends the request on a journey

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.580
across the internet.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.810
Often, it's the journey
back where things can

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.700
get a little complicated and crowded.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.410
Imagine a network of highways

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.690
with delivery trucks running across it.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.020
Each of these are packets,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.620
the basic unit of internet traffic.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.060
Some are Amazon, some are Netflix

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.100
and some might be a photo
you're e-mailing to your aunt.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.900
And they're all taking different routes

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.190
based on agreements the
networks have with each other.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.920
Your video might be passed
from one network to another

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.960
until it gets back to you.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.910
Or the content provider
might pay a transit provider

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:18.290
to carry the request back.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.020
Either way, it's sharing the road

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:21.870
with a lot of other content.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:23.610
So there are plenty of places were things

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:24.713
can get jammed up.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.354
Anytime one network hands
off content to another

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:30.720
there's potential for a traffic jam,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.120
which can slow down your video

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.633
and cause a spinning wheel.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.060
The important thing to understand

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.020
is that you can't fix
a bottleneck out here

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.100
by paying your provider for higher speeds

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.200
within your own network.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.880
Your network is only
responsible for this leg

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.400
of the journey.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.290
Paying for faster internet just because

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.750
you encountered a spinning wheel

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.760
is kind of like getting a bigger driveway

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.320
in hopes you'll get to work faster.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.990
So, what does all of this
look like in real life?

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.110
In real life, these networks are made of

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.810
millions of miles of fiber and cable

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.670
running around the world and connecting

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.603
with each other in big data
centers full of servers.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:11.870
Here's an actual request for the homepage

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.170
of a local broadcaster in New Zealand,

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:17.770
provided to us by researchers
at The University of Chicago.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.740
It starts in Vienna, Virginia,

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.320
outside of Washington D.C.
with a Verizon Fios user

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.083
and travels across the
country on Verizon's network.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.870
In California, it connects
to a different network

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.510
which sends it to Austin, Texas

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.123
and then back to California.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.630
It then gets handed to a transit provider

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.510
which sends it across the Pacific Ocean

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:40.240
to Auckland, New Zealand.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.310
It likely takes a similar path back.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.623
See all the opportunities
for traffic jams?

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:49.840
Other trips have few or even no hand offs.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.060
Powerful tech companies like
Google, Netflix and Amazon

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.990
and content delivery networks
have rolled out thousands

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:57.680
of servers spanning the globe

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.710
to get copies of popular
content closer to you.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.300
Smaller sites can rent space
from some of these companies

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.010
to speed up their journey.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.210
Here's a request for netflix.com

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.650
that starts in Los Angeles, California.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.820
It goes to a server in
Calipatria, California.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:13.653
That's it.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.950
So why do you sometimes
get a spinning wheel

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.750
when you request content from Netflix

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.890
or other big tech companies?

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:23.090
Well, sometimes their servers can crash

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:24.300
and cause an outage,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.110
or maybe they took a longer
route that got congested.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.750
But the problem could be closer to home.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:33.220
Are you trying to stream video
during peak evening hours?

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:34.110
In many networks

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.790
you're sharing bandwidth
with your neighbors.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.840
Or maybe it's your WiFi.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.130
Maybe you're leasing an
out of date modem or router

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.910
from your provider that isn't capable

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:45.060
of receiving the speeds you're paying for.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.180
It might be worth an upgrade.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.150
You may also have an interference problem

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.180
from another device, like
your neighbor's baby monitor

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.460
or maybe you're just too
far away from your router.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.940
Or it just could be your own device.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:00.300
Are you using an old laptop or phone,

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.330
or an outdated streaming box?

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:04.780
Your device could be slowing you down.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.950
It could help to restart
your modem or router

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.060
or even invest in a better one.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.750
Same for your devices.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.690
But a lot is still out of your control.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.410
Like if your neighbor's son is downloading

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.300
15 video games one night.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.830
Or a traffic jam out in the internet.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:21.750
So next time this happens
and you call your provider,

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.550
they'll likely try to sell you more speed

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:24.850
for your connection,

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.323
but now you know better.

